-*- coding:utf-8 -*-

bricscad-com-bridge-booting — BricsCAD vendor probes never leave BOOTING
========================================================================

Reported from: the Windows vendor-probe CI job
(gitlab job 15534582488, vendor:probes:bricscad) against BricsCAD on the
Windows runner. All three probes: "no verdict (backend unreachable or
crashed)".

Diagnosis (from --keep-workdir artifacts)
-----------------------------------------

alfe's own log (per probe):
    backend BRICSCAD: spawned, process-info-pid = 37028
    protocol: status now "BOOTING" (elapsed 0.00 s)
    protocol: timeout after 30.30 s (last status "BOOTING")
    BricsCAD did not reach READY within 30 s (last status: "BOOTING"). [READY-TIMEOUT]

protocol/status.txt = BOOTING ; logs/debug.log EMPTY ; stderr.txt EMPTY.

So the alfe-side is healthy: it staged the runtime, emitted
bridge-bricscad.vbs, spawned `cscript bridge.vbs`, and polled
status.txt. But run-common.lsp NEVER executed on the BricsCAD side
(debug.log is empty, not merely late), and the VBS wrote no COM error.
i.e. BricsCAD launched (or attached) but the `(load run-common.lsp)`
dispatched by doc.SendCommand never ran. Two plausible causes, not
distinguishable from the artifacts as they were:

  a. Cold-start slower than the 30 s READY timeout (COM server + license
     + first document), so the queued load hadn't run when alfe gave up.
  b. A modal dialog blocking SendCommand -- most likely BricsCAD's LISP
     load-security prompt (SECURELOAD > 0) on a .lsp under C:/msys64/tmp,
     or a startup/license dialog.

The bridge VBS had a debugFile hook (VBSDebug) but the caller never
passed :debug-path, so every kept workdir was blind on the VBS side --
the missing evidence to tell (a) from (b).

Fix (alfe 1.6.5) — observability + both mitigations
---------------------------------------------------

autolisp-front-end/source/backend-bricscad.lisp
  * emit-bridge-vbs call now passes :debug-path <workdir>/bridge-vbs.log,
    so --keep-workdir captures how far the bridge got (attach vs create,
    app name/version, doc count, whether SendCommand was dispatched, any
    COM error). Added VBSDebug traces throughout the template.
  * bridge: after a *created* (cold) BricsCAD, WScript.Sleep 3000 to let
    the document/command context settle before SendCommand (an early
    SendCommand can be silently dropped).
  * bridge: SendCommand "(setvar \"SECURELOAD\" 0)" before the load, so
    the temp-dir runtime loads without a modal security prompt (cause b).
  * start-engine now lets --timeout / $AUTOLISP_WAIT_SECS raise the READY
    timeout (was a hard-coded 30 s, unreachable from the CLI) -- covers
    cause a.

scripts/run-vendor-probes.ps1
  * passes --timeout 180 for the bricscad backend (cold-start budget).

Follow-up (alfe 1.6.7) — drop the VBS: Windows uses batch mode too
------------------------------------------------------------------

pjb's observation: `alfe --bricscad --mode batch` works well on macOS, so
why go through VBScript/COM on Windows at all? It needn't. BricsCAD batch
is `bricscad.exe -B run.scr` -- the SAME GUI-exe + script mechanism
macOS/Linux already use -- and it sidesteps the whole COM surface
(SendCommand timing, the modal SECURELOAD/startup dialog that swallowed
the load). Windows only used automation because choose-effective-mode
hardcoded :auto -> :automation there.

Changes:
  * choose-effective-mode: :auto now picks :batch on EVERY platform when
    bricscad.exe is found (else :automation). detect already resolves the
    Windows exe (glob or $BRICSCAD_EXE), so the batch path has its binary.
  * emit-run-scr: prepend a guarded (setvar "SECURELOAD" 0) so batch mode
    is not blocked by the load-security prompt either.
  * scripts/run-vendor-probes.ps1: bricscad probes pass --mode batch
    (+ --timeout 180 for cold GUI start).
  * The VBScript automation path remains, reachable via --mode automation,
    now instrumented (bridge-vbs.log) from the earlier round if ever needed.

Likely root cause: BricsCAD never reaches a command-ready state (licensing)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Batch mode (1.6.7) DID launch the BricsCAD GUI on the Windows runner, but
it too sat at BOOTING -- run.scr never executed. pjb noted BricsCAD may be
waiting on the license server with the VPN possibly down (infra he does
not fully control).

Decisive deduction: BOTH launch paths stall identically at BOOTING --
  * automation/COM: CreateObject succeeds, SendCommand dispatched with NO
    error, yet the (load) never runs;
  * batch: bricscad.exe -B run.scr opens the GUI, yet run.scr never runs.
The COM path does not use -B at all, so a -B switch problem cannot be the
common cause. The one thing common to both is that BricsCAD launches but
never reaches a command-ready prompt, so neither the queued SendCommand
nor the -B script fires. A license-checkout block during startup (no VPN
-> license server unreachable) fits exactly, and also retroactively
explains the original automation BOOTING failures.

=> This is environmental, NOT an alfe defect. The alfe-side work stands
(batch is the right default; the COM path is instrumented as a fallback).
Do not chase it further as a code bug until a *licensed* BricsCAD can
reach its command prompt on the runner.

Update: licensed now, and it's the Windows script switch (-B vs /b)
------------------------------------------------------------------

The license/VPN did come up: on the batch run BricsCAD opened and showed
the (empty) drawing -- i.e. it reached a command-ready GUI -- yet run.scr
STILL never ran and it timed out at BOOTING. Command-ready but script not
executing rules out licensing as the remaining cause and points at the
launch switch: build-launch-argv passed `-B run.scr` on every platform,
but the Windows (AutoCAD-lineage) BricsCAD CLI takes `/b`, not the Unix
`-B`. With `-B`, Windows BricsCAD opens the GUI and silently ignores the
script.

Fix (alfe 1.6.8):
  * build-launch-argv batch: use "/b" on Windows, "-B" elsewhere.
  * emit-run-scr: first action now drops a run-scr-started.txt marker, so
    the next kept workdir is self-diagnosing:
      - marker present + debug.log filled -> script ran, runtime advanced;
      - marker present + debug.log empty  -> the (load) is the problem;
      - marker absent                     -> the script switch STILL isn't
        firing (try another form).

Status
------

OPEN pending the next vendor:probes:bricscad run (needs a licensed
BricsCAD, which the runner now has). Read run-scr-started.txt +
logs/debug.log in the kept workdir per the table above.
